WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.700
YUFENG GUO: In this episode
of Cloud AI Adventures,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.550
find out how to train on
increasingly complex datasets

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:09.380
by converting a linear model
to a deep neural network.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.930
As the number of feature
columns in a linear model grows,

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.690
it can be increasingly difficult
to achieve high accuracies

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.730
in your training
as the interactions

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.680
between different
columns get more complex.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.030
This is a known problem, and one
particularly effective solution

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:28.350
for data scientists to use
is deep neural networks.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.680
Deep neural networks are able to
adapt to more complex datasets

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.220
and better generalize to
previously unseen data because

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.000
of its multiple layers.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.700
Hence why they're called deep.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.940
These layers allow them to
fit more complex datasets

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.530
than linear models can.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.620
However, the trade-off is
that the model will generally

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.460
tend to take longer to
train, be larger in size,

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.020
and have less interpretability.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.900
So why would anyone
want to use it?

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.110
Because it can lead to
higher final accuracies

00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:59.787
on this complicated dataset.

00:00:59.787 --> 00:01:01.620
One of the tricky things
about deep learning

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.290
is that getting all the
parameters to be just right

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.540
can be very tough.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.790
Depending on your dataset,
the configurations

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.680
can seem virtually limitless.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.970
However, TensorFlow's
built-in deep classifier

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.940
and deep regressor classes
supply a reasonable set

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.250
of defaults that you can
get started with right away,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.330
allowing you to get
going quickly and easily.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.480
Let's see an example of
how to update our Iris

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.900
example from a linear model to
a deep neural network, often

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.780
abbreviated as DNN.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:37.950
Now, we don't have time in
this video to properly show off

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:41.670
a 2,000-column model that
a DNN would leverage,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.520
so I'm just going to use the
four that we've been using

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.140
throughout this series.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.870
The main change comes from
replacing our linear classifier

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.060
class with DNN classifier.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.030
This will create a deep
neural network for us.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.580
Virtually everything
else remains the same.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.960
There is one
additional parameter

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.793
that is required for
the deep neural network

00:01:59.793 --> 00:02:01.620
that we did not
include previously.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.780
Since deep neural networks
have multiple layers,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.780
and each layer has potentially
a different number of nodes,

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.970
we'll add a hidden
units argument.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.590
The hidden units
argument allows you

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:14.699
to supply an array with the
number of nodes for each layer.

00:02:14.699 --> 00:02:16.740
This allows you to create
a neural network simply

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.080
by considering
its size and shape

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.690
rather than writing the whole
thing from scratch by hand

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.290
and wiring everything up.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:26.640
Adding or removing layers is
as simple as adding or removing

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.120
an element in the array.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.720
Of course, with any
preconstructed system,

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.710
what you gain in
convenience, you often

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.570
lose in customizability.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.820
DNN classifier tries
to get around this

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.160
by including a number
of additional parameters

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.170
that you can optionally use.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:46.980
When left out, some regional
bold defaults are utilized.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.920
For example, the optimizer,
the activation function,

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.190
and dropout ratios are all
available to customize, along

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:54.510
with many others.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.100
What else do you
have to do in order

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.760
to convert our model
from linear to deep?

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:00.810
Nothing.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.240
That's the beauty of using
the estimators framework,

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.380
one common way to organize your
data, training, evaluation,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.650
and model exporting, while
still giving you the flexibility

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.690
to experiment with
different models

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.160
and different parameters.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.180
Sometimes, deep neural networks
can outperform linear models.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.310
In these situations,
TensorFlow makes

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.370
it easy to switch from a linear
model to a deep one with very

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.050
little code changes
by leveraging

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.560
the estimators framework to
replace just one function call.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.680
This means more time to work on
the data, model, and parameters

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.500
rather than wiring up
your training loop.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.870
I'm Yufeng Guo, and if
you want to learn more, be

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.320
sure to subscribe
to the channel,

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.740
and check out the rest
of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.380
Don't forget to use
TensorFlow estimators

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.060
for easy deep neural networks.

